I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and diffraction profile first, then use the available phase-search and GSAS-II tooling to identify candidate structures, refine them jointly, and validate that the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and project all agree. I’ll keep you posted as the phase model converges.

The strongest cluster at 23–25° already points to crystalline tungsten oxide rather than a single barium tungstate product, while additional peaks may represent a barium-bearing phase. I’m now separating overlapping peak families and searching chemically plausible database structures before refinement.

The database search confirms two crystalline families: tungsten(VI) oxide and tetragonal barium peroxide. No independent BaWO₄ peak set is present (its diagnostic 31.9° line is absent). The first trial also exposed an exact scale degeneracy in GSAS-II’s reflection defaults; I have fixed the redundant overall scale and am now comparing the plausible WO₃ polymorphs with constrained, phase-by-phase cell refinement.

The constrained monoclinic WO₃ trial is still refining across the full 10–100° range. I’m retaining the measured Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ wavelengths and ratio, fixing only the scale redundancy, and testing peak-width terms sequentially so the phase fractions are not absorbed into an unstable profile model.

The peak-family assignment is holding up: tetragonal BaO₂ explains the 26.3°, 27.1°, 43.0°, and 46.6° reflections, while monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the diagnostic 23–25° triplet and most higher-angle structure. The remaining comparison is between two crystallographically related WO₃ models; their peak positions are similar, but their relative intensities and refined stability differ enough to affect the quantitative fraction.

The anisotropic broadening test was numerically unstable and tried to suppress the clearly visible BaO₂ phase, so I’m rejecting that branch. I’m returning to the stable two-phase Rietveld model and separating lattice/zero refinement from peak-shape refinement; this keeps the quantitative scale factors tied to integrated intensities rather than compensating through pathological widths.

I’ve narrowed the pattern to monoclinic WO₃ plus tetragonal BaO₂, with no convincing BaWO₄ reflections. I’m making one final like-for-like comparison between two published room-temperature WO₃ coordinate sets, then I’ll export and validate the GSAS-II project and all requested deliverables.

The transformed neutron-derived WO₃ model gives a marginally better whole-pattern fit (Rwp 14.969% versus 15.004%) and converges cleanly; its refined phase split is 64.97 wt% WO₃ / 35.03 wt% BaO₂. I’m now checking whether the remaining intensity residuals justify any physically constrained texture/coordinate refinement, or should instead be reported as model uncertainty.

A constrained coordinate-descent refinement of crystallite size and microstrain improved Rwp from 14.97% to 13.67% without hitting bounds or destabilizing the fractions. The physically interpretable endpoints are about 49 nm/0.64% strain for WO₃ and 99 nm/1.05% for BaO₂; I’m doing a final convergence pass and then will freeze these nuisance terms for the reported quantitative fit.

The remaining misfit is dominated by anisotropic line widths in monoclinic WO₃—especially the sharp 23–25° triplet versus broader higher-angle groups—not by unexplained peak positions. I’m testing a bounded Stephens strain model against the simpler isotropic profile; I’ll keep it only if it converges cleanly and materially improves the fit without changing the phase assignment.

The anisotropic-width correction is stable, and only very mild preferred orientation is indicated (March–Dollase factors ≈1.03 for both phases). I’m consolidating those terms in one bounded refinement, then re-polishing lattice metrics and zero offset so the final project is internally consistent rather than a sequence of frozen trial endpoints.

The consolidated refinement converged with no frozen variables at Rwp 12.64%, Rp 9.25%, and GOF 3.47. The modeled crystalline fractions are 64.58 wt% WO₃ and 35.42 wt% BaO₂; I’m now exporting the exact project state and validating that the CIFs, profile CSV, and JSON all reproduce those values.

All requested files are now written. I caught and corrected a one-grid-point boundary mismatch so `fit.csv` contains exactly the same 8,284 observations used by GSAS-II; the remaining check is independent CIF parsing plus numerical round-trip verification against `final.gpx`.

